Z||000000||sqoach||07-22-2001||06:11 PM||||Just about every Fiero I've seen in person (which is not very many) have scratches all the way up the window. What causes this? I was told by someone that it was the dew wipes, but in the places it looks like it would scratch the window, it doesn't, and vice versa. And the scratches are always in the same spot it seems. What causes this? Is it just a really bad design? I'm probably gonna replace my windows, but I don't want this to happen again. It would just defeat the purpose. Thanks for any help. I did a search, but didn't really come up with anything.<P>------------------<BR><FONT COLOR="BLUE">sqoach@yahoo.com</FONT><BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sqoach.gif">   <P>84 SE (FOR SALE)*sale pending*<BR>85 Coupe (parts car)($50)*sale pending*<BR>86 SC (FOR SALE)<BR>86 SE V6 (new project)||||reg||9
Z||000001||Mach10||07-22-2001||06:20 PM||||Don't know, but I think I may be able to fix 'em. Once I find a "test" window, I'll break out the die-grinder with cloth wheel and some jewellers rouge. I'm hoping that it doesn't cause any serious distortion...||||reg||7
Z||000002||California Kid||07-22-2001||06:43 PM||||It's the Dew Wipe Strips, sometimes called outer belt glass sealing strip. There are 4 cloth padded areas that are part of the strip you can't see (below the exposed rubber strip/in the inner door cavity) that become dirt/grit impregnated. If it's bad enough you'll see small vertical scratch lines on the outer glass. If it's a disaster, the cloth pad is worn thru and now you have metal against glass.<P>Best thing to do is replace them, they last a very long time (time them this long to get screwed up). "The Fiero Store" carries them, no real cheap and a pain to replace.<P>Don't worry about your inner belt seals unless you're starting to see scratches on the inner window. These very seldom good bad as they aren't exposed to very much contamination.||||reg||1
